Yes, but extend this even more.  For example, it is already possible
to train your pet to loot shops.  Why not extend the training bit
by allowing you to train your pet to "come", "sit", "stay", heel, and,
most important, "sic".  ;^)

How about training him to fetch?  All your expended missile weapons
would be returned to you by a well-trained pet.  You fire off all your
arrows, then you shout "fetch" and you pet runs around the room,
retrieving all your arrows, and drops them at your feet.  Especially
nice if your pet is speeded and you are not.

Training would consist of throwing something in an empty room, then
waiting till your pet fetches it.  Then reward him with tripe,
followed by yelling "fetch".  This might require several training
sessions, depending on your intrinsics.

Stay would be useful on those levels where you know there is a level
teleport trap or trap doors, or a zoo.  Also, once your pet is trained
to sit and to stay (both used together), you can tell him to "sit",
followed by "stay", and then leave him on a level and as long as you
return before he starves he will still be your loyal pet.  A pet that
is "stay"-ing will not rush to eat tripe rations.  He will only eat
whatever food you throw to him.  You must call him to release him.

Training for stay would require you to be present some time when your
pet falls into a pit.  You stand a couple of squares away and toss
tripe to him while yelling "stay".

"Come" would require an uncursed whistle.  Yell "come", blow the whistle,
then reward him with tripe.  Once trained the whistle could be discarded.

Heel would require a leash to train the pet.  Once trained the leash
may be discarded.

A ring of conflict might be used to train the pet to sic.

The actual word used to train your pet can be anything.  It will be
whatever word you use to tie into the behavior you are training.  This
way you may have more than one pet and you can have them trained to
different command words.

